{"rules":[{"jurisdiction":"NJ","level":"state","category":"just_cause_eviction","status":"in_force","title":"New Jersey Anti-Eviction Act (Eviction Law): good-cause eviction grounds and notices to quit (this part: subsections (n)-(r), owner-occupied 2-3 family dwellings, foreclosure)","requirement":"A landlord may remove a tenant only on statutory good cause and by court order. This part covers the following grounds. (n) Drug offenses by the tenant or by a person the tenant lets occupy the unit. (o) Assault or terrorist threats against the landlord, the landlord's family or employees. (p) Civil-court liability for theft, assault or threats, or drug activity. (q) Theft from the landlord or from tenants in the same building. (r) Human trafficking. Under (n), (o) and (p), no suit may be brought more than two years after the juvenile adjudication, the conviction or the release from incarceration, whichever is later. Under (r), no suit may be brought more than two years after the violation ended. Under (n)-(q), a Notice to Quit must be served at least 3 days before suit. In landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings, eviction requires a court order and proof of one of six causes. These are: staying after the lease term expires, non-payment of rent, disorderly conduct, willful damage, constant violation of written rules, or violation of a lease provision with a right of re-entry. Notice to quit: 3 months for at-will or year-to-year tenancies, 1 month for month-to-month tenancies, and 3 days for the disorderly, destructive or rule/lease-violation causes. Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction. Before evicting after a foreclosure sale to an owner-occupant buyer, a 90-day notice to quit is required, and a lease running past 90 days must expire first. Reprisal eviction is barred (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10). Penalties: if the landlord says it will personally occupy the unit and then lets another tenant occupy it or registers a condominium/cooperative conversion, it is liable for treble damages plus fees. If the landlord says it will demolish or retire the property, there can be no residential use for 5 years, with treble damages and a civil penalty of up to $10,000 per violation.","key_value":"3-day notice to quit (grounds n-q); 3-month / 1-month notices for owner-occupied 2-3 family; 90-day notice after foreclosure sale; 2-year limit for suits","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies generally. Owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings have a separate, shorter list of causes. Rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements. Public housing authorities must follow State law plus HUD rules.","exemptions":"Reprisal protection excludes owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings. Hotels, dormitories and certain other buildings are excluded from the rooming/boarding house definition. This part states no rent-increase coverage cut-offs.","coverage_logic":{"covers":null,"exempt":null},"subject":"landlord","interaction":"Federal foreclosure law does not preempt State or local law that gives tenants longer time periods or more protection. Public housing authorities must follow both State law and HUD regulations.","yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":null,"effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":null,"end_date":null,"penalty":"Treble damages plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000 per violation (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6)","citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1 et seq.","quoted_span":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction in New Jersey.","supporting_spans":["Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction."],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_note":null},{"jurisdiction":"NJ","level":"state","category":"just_cause_eviction","status":"in_force","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act (condominium/cooperative conversion) and Tenant Protection Act of 1992 (Hudson County)","requirement":"Eligible senior, disabled or disabled-veteran tenants in a building being converted to a condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership may be protected from eviction for 40 years. Eligibility: age 62+ before the conversion recording date, or permanently disabled, or honorably discharged and 60%+ service-disabled. The tenant must have lived in the building at least 1 year before the conversion recording date. Family income must not exceed the greater of 3 times the average per-person county income or $50,000. The landlord or converter must notify tenants of their right to file. Applications are generally due within 60 days. The 1992 Tenant Protection Act extends protections to qualified tenants in Hudson County, the only qualified county.","key_value":"40 years protected tenancy; 60-day application window; income test of 3x county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Buildings converted or being converted to a condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership. Qualified tenants only. The 1992 Act applies only in Hudson County.","exemptions":null,"coverage_logic":{"covers":{"field":"other_fact","op":"==","value":"building being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exempt":null},"subject":"landlord","interaction":null,"yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":false,"effective_date":null,"effective_rule":null,"enacted_date":null,"end_date":null,"penalty":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"The Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act protects senior citizens who meet certain eligibility requirements (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39; N.J.A.C. 5:24-2.1 to -2.11).","supporting_spans":["At the present time, the only qualified county is Hudson County (N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.2(b))."],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_note":null}],"notes":"Part 4 of 4 contains no operative text on rent-increase limits (rent control appears only as a pressure-tactic reference in the Foreclosure Fairness Act), security deposits, screening fees, screening restrictions or algorithmic pricing. The Anti-Eviction Act coverage cut-offs (such as the owner-occupied 1-3 unit exemption) were not stated as rent-control conditions in this part, so no rent_increase_limits record was created. The rest of this part is contact lists and agency directories, which are not rules."}
